#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060552} :activity/id #uuid "29cf2698-b761-565d-9655-9c36bff61aab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060984 :tutorial/content "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$ \n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B\n$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as \n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.* \n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form. \n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:  \n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." :tutorial/id #uuid "6634a172-a5c9-557f-9091-35a4ebd198d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7978 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "082980d1-6f7a-54ee-8a3f-6ce0b731dc29" :step/math-academy-id 139680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059409 :question/id #uuid "48364248-d296-502c-96da-d1905be35627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411063}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "9889ed7d-e94e-5481-9500-3dcd8edb1f23" :step/math-academy-id 139681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059425 :question/id #uuid "54512495-0ca8-55a3-834d-0b70f9